  `<spec-folder>`\\n================== references/providers-and-models.md\\n- Core Principle\\n+ Purpose\\n- This mode is single-provider (OpenAI via ChatGPT OAuth) — the choice space is one provider, five models, and one effort ladder capped per model.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ This mode is single-provider (OpenAI via ChatGPT OAuth); the choice space is one provider, five models, and one effort ladder capped per model.\\n- §6),\\n+ [../SKILL.md](../SKILL.md) §4 Rule 10),\\n- default\\n- medium`; raise\\n+ medium`.\\n- planning; escalate\\n+ planning.\\n- `xhigh` —\\n+ `xhigh`:\\n- review; drop\\n+ review.\\n- `plan_mode_reasoning_effort` —\\n+ `plan_mode_reasoning_effort`;\\n+ `codex exec` defaults to `--sandbox read-only`, so file-modification tasks silently no-op unless you add `--sandbox workspace-write` (or `--full-auto`). See [integration-patterns.md](./integration-patterns.md).\\n- `codex exec` defaults to `--sandbox read-only`, so file-modification tasks silently no-op unless you add `--sandbox workspace-write` (or `--full-auto`). See [integration-patterns.md](./integration-patterns.md).\\n\"}],\"providerCall\":{\"executed\":false},\"providerResult\":{\"executed\":false},\"rawInput\":\"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/315-frontend-reference-restructure && for f in ROUTER.md SKILL.md assets/prompt-quality-card.md assets/prompt-templates.md references/agent-delegation.md references/cli-reference.md references/codex-tools.md references/hook-contract.md references/providers-and-models.md; do echo \\\\\\\"================== $f\\\\\\\"; git diff --word-diff=porcelain -U0 -- \\\\\\\".skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/$f\\\\\\\" | grep -E '^[-+][^-+]' | sed 's/^\\\\\\\\([-+]\\\\\\\\)/\\\\\\\\1 /' | head -120; done\\\"}\"},\"time\":{\"start\":1793796527609,\"end\":1793796528657}}}}\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:475:+> The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\"...\"]` as a\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:476:+> `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:494:-> The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\"...\"]` as a\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:495:-> `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/deep-research-strategy.md:46:- q6-external-refs: **Four live `.opencode`-naming references outside the repo, plus a surviving path-keyed class.** Live: `~/.config/git/hooks/*` (7 symlinks → main checkout), `~/.codex/hooks.json` (18 command strings), `~/.hermes/config.yaml` MCP args, `~/.codex/config.toml` `[projects.\"…/Public/.opencode\"]` trust entry. Survives: registries keyed on the checkout root (`~/.codex` projects + hooks.state, `~/.hermes` trusted_project_dirs, `~/.claude.json`), `~/.pi/agent/*` → `.pi/*` symlinks, `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. Clean negatives named. No in-repo validator checks this class. (iteration 4)\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-004.md:73:- `~/.codex/config.toml` `[projects.\"…/Public\"]` trust entry and all `[hooks.state.\"…/.codex/hooks.json:…\"]` keys [SOURCE: ~/.codex/config.toml:15,115-417]\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/research.md:72:Four live `.opencode`-naming references, none of which moves with `git mv`: the seven git-hook symlinks; `~/.codex/hooks.json` (18 `.opencode` command strings, installed outbound by `install-codex-hooks.mjs`); `~/.hermes/config.yaml`'s `mcp_servers.code_mode` args; and `~/.codex/config.toml`'s `[projects.\"…/Public/.opencode\"]` trust entry [SOURCE: iteration-004.md F4.7]. The surviving class keys on the checkout root instead: `~/.codex` `[projects.\"…/Public\"]` and `[hooks.state.*]`, `~/.hermes` `trusted_project_dirs`, `~/.claude.json`, `~/.pi/agent/*` → `.pi/*` symlinks, `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. Clean negatives were checked and named (`~/.claude/settings.json`, `~/.config/devin/*`, `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `~/.claude/plugins/`); `~/.zshrc`'s `$HOME/.opencode/bin` entry is stale (directory absent). **No in-repo validator covers this class** — the inventory is the whole safety net [SOURCE: iteration-004.md F4.8].\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/findings-registry.json:8:    {\"id\": \"q6-external-refs\", \"text\": \"Which references live outside the repository and will silently keep pointing at the old path?\", \"resolvedAt\": \"iteration-4\", \"answer\": \"Four live .opencode-naming references: 7 git-hook symlinks (~/.config/git/hooks → main checkout), ~/.codex/hooks.json (18 command strings), ~/.hermes/config.yaml mcp_servers args, ~/.codex/config.toml [projects.\\\".../Public/.opencode\\\"] trust entry. Survives (checkout-root-keyed): ~/.codex projects + hooks.state, ~/.hermes trusted_project_dirs, ~/.claude.json, ~/.pi/agent/* → .pi/* symlinks, ~/.codex/AGENTS.md. Clean negatives: ~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.config/devin/*, ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, ~/.claude/plugins. ~/.zshrc $HOME/.opencode/bin entry is stale (dir absent). No in-repo validator covers this class.\"},\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/codex-hooks-client.mjs.txt:3://   node codex-hooks.mjs trust <cwd> <key>...  writes hooks.state.\"<key>\".trusted_hash = the hash Codex reports now,\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/codex-hooks-client.mjs.txt:54:      return { keyPath: `hooks.state.\"${key}\".trusted_hash`, mergeStrategy: 'upsert', value: h.currentHash };\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:4:< trusted_hash = \"sha256:024cc055c67890d2d436364490bacaf8bee2c8224efdf66add12889091efd082\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:6:> trusted_hash = \"sha256:0514fa8861fa752349708dd1d0ee0167c9333984d1f2bf93b3c8e5fb5be0b2aa\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:8:< trusted_hash = \"sha256:0514fa8861fa752349708dd1d0ee0167c9333984d1f2bf93b3c8e5fb5be0b2aa\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:10:> trusted_hash = \"sha256:b9674f1ef82659aecbd346ee56c3c6d6fc9327df619774917e78c0b2b134ab64\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:12:> [hooks.state.\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/.codex/hooks.json:session_start:0:1\"]\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:13:> trusted_hash = \"sha256:87545efe882d91c73e06a6216984f32c339e4cc5f1bc2d5c3039146246b2aca5\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/implementation-summary.md:227:   Codex keys hook trust by position: `~/.codex/config.toml` holds a `trusted_hash` for each `<file>:<event>:<group>:<hook>`. Deleting the first SessionStart group moved every later one up a place, so Codex now treats the global SessionStart hooks as new or changed and skips them. A plain `codex exec` from the checkout started 6 SessionStart hooks, the project file's count, where 16 ran before. The installer moves none of the hooks it keeps. Run it first, then start `codex` once in a terminal and trust the hooks its startup review lists, or trust them later with `/hooks`. Expect three: nodeterm, GitKraken and orca. Trusting before the installer run would also re-trust the six SessionStart copies, and every session would run them twice again until the installer removes them. To undo the jcode deletion instead, copy the backup back over `~/.codex/hooks.json`.\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/spec.md:99:$ rg -n \"trusted_hash\" .codex/README.md\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/spec.md:100:54:2. Approve the hooks. Codex runs a project hook only after you approve that exact entry. Open `/hooks` in an interactive session and approve each entry it lists for review. The approval is stored as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again.\n\n[showing lines 20-40 of 40; full output saved to /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/opencode/shell/4c10847297631c3af43d766cf1908920fe00c449/sh_1d22a3b7b001SAdr4X8qAzfn4H.out]","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":true,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/review-1.md.json:84:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1793796371522,\"sessionID\":\"ses_e596b6c59ffeYFKlEpeH4Zk8ze\",\"part\":{\"partID\":\"prt_1a698728f001AFsH0DaIk8aOvs\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_e596b6c59ffeYFKlEpeH4Zk8ze\",\"messageID\":\"msg_1a69804940014Mw7wZ5r2N9UGa\",\"type\":\"tool\",\"id\":\"call_00_mcpa9jz7hi3kyrq9ul1w4a2s\",\"tool\":\"shell\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/315-frontend-reference-restructure && git show HEAD:.skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/hook-contract.md | sed -n '60,135p' | nl -ba -v60\"},\"output\":\"    60\\t    ],\\n    61\\t    \\\"UserPromptSubmit\\\": [\\n    62\\t      {\\n    63\\t        \\\"hooks\\\": [\\n    64\\t          {\\n    65\\t            \\\"type\\\": \\\"command\\\",\\n    66\\t            \\\"command\\\": \\\"node /absolute/path/to/user-prompt-submit.js\\\",\\n    67\\t            \\\"timeout\\\": 3\\n    68\\t          }\\n    69\\t        ]\\n    70\\t      }\\n    71\\t    ]\\n    72\\t  }\\n    73\\t}\\n    74\\t```\\n    75\\t\\n    76\\tWhen appending Spec Kit hooks, preserve existing Superset `notify.sh`\\n    77\\tentries. Multiple hooks for one event run concurrently, so hook commands must be\\n    78\\tidempotent and must not rely on registration order.\\n    79\\t\\n    80\\t### 3.1 Tool-Level Guard Adapters (Claude / OpenCode parity)\\n    81\\t\\n    82\\tBeyond the session-lifecycle hooks, Codex mirrors the repo's guard hooks as thin\\n    83\\tadapters over the same runtime-neutral cores the Claude hooks and OpenCode plugins\\n    84\\tuse. Each fails open (exit 0 on empty/malformed stdin); the two deny-capable guards\\n    85\\temit `hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision: \\\"deny\\\"`, which Codex honors.\\n    86\\t\\n    87\\t| Event · matcher | Guard | Adapter |\\n    88\\t|---|---|---|\\n    89\\t| PreToolUse · `exec\\\\|Bash\\\\|apply_patch\\\\|edit` | spec-gate enforce (deny-capable) | `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/codex/spec-gate-enforce.mjs` |\\n    90\\t| UserPromptSubmit | spec-gate classify (state only, no emit) | `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/codex/spec-gate-classify.mjs` |\\n    91\\t| PreToolUse · `exec\\\\|Bash` | dispatch preflight lint (deny-capable) | `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-preflight-lint.mjs` |\\n    92\\t| PostToolUse · `apply_patch\\\\|edit` | post-edit quality | `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/codex/post-edit-quality.cjs` |\\n    93\\t| PostToolUse · `exec\\\\|Bash` | dispatch audit (observe) | `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-audit-posttooluse.mjs` |\\n    94\\t| Stop | completion-evidence sentinel (advisory) | `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/codex/completion-evidence-stop.cjs` |\\n    95\\t| PreToolUse · `mcp__.*` | mcp route guard (dormant until an external MCP family registers) | `.skilled/hooks/mcp-route-guard/codex/mcp-route-guard.cjs` |\\n    96\\t\\n    97\\t> Codex delivers an `apply_patch` target path inside `tool_input.command` (the\\n    98\\t> `*** Add/Update/Delete File:` header), not a `file_path` field — filePath-driven\\n    99\\t> adapters parse the path from the patch body. A Stop hook's stdout is parsed as a\\n   100\\t> response envelope, so neutral shell scripts wired to Stop must not emit stdout.\\n   101\\t\\n   102\\t> Codex 0.160 renamed its shell tool from `exec` to `Bash`, and a matcher of\\n   103\\t> `exec` alone no longer fires for a shell call. Every shell matcher names both.\\n   104\\t> The same release hands PostToolUse the shell output as one string where older\\n   105\\t> builds handed `{stdout, stderr}`, so the audit adapter reads either shape.\\n   106\\t\\n   107\\t> Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\\n   108\\t> The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\\\"...\\\"]` as a\\n   109\\t> `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\\n   110\\t> the approval is a hash, a changed entry is likely to need approving again. After a\\n   111\\t> `hooks.json` change, open `/hooks` in an interactive Codex session and approve every\\n   112\\t> entry it lists as needing review.\\n   113\\t\\n   114\\t> Codex has no task-dispatch guard. Its `spawn_agent` call reaches PreToolUse as\\n   115\\t> `collaborationspawn_agent`, but the hook payload carries the spawn `message`\\n   116\\t> encrypted (`gAAAAA...`) beside `task_name` and `fork_turns`, so the guard has no\\n   117\\t> prompt or target to check.\\n   118\\t\\n   119\\tCodex loads the full repo hook set (lifecycle and guards) from `.codex/hooks.json`.\\n   120\\tThe user-global `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of it, or every hook runs\\n   121\\ttwice. `.skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` removes such copies, backs up first,\\n   122\\tand keeps Superset and other third-party entries.\\n   123\\t\\n   124\\t---\\n   125\\t\\n   126\\t## 4. EVENT INPUTS\\n   127\\t\\n   128\\tHook commands receive JSON on stdin. Current generated schemas use snake_case\\n   129\\tfield names and reject unknown properties.\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t`SessionStart` required shape:\\n   132\\t\\n   133\\t```json\\n   134\\t{\\n   135\\t  \\\"session_id\\\": \\\"uuid\\\",\\n\",\"title\":\"shell\",\"metadata\":{\"metadata\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit\":0},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"    60\\t    ],\\n    61\\t    \\\"UserPromptSubmit\\\": [\\n    62\\t      {\\n    63\\t        \\\"hooks\\\": [\\n    64\\t          {\\n    65\\t            \\\"type\\\": \\\"command\\\",\\n    66\\t            \\\"command\\\": \\\"node /absolute/path/to/user-prompt-submit.js\\\",\\n    67\\t            \\\"timeout\\\": 3\\n    68\\t          }\\n    69\\t        ]\\n    70\\t      }\\n    71\\t    ]\\n    72\\t  }\\n    73\\t}\\n    74\\t```\\n    75\\t\\n    76\\tWhen appending Spec Kit hooks, preserve existing Superset `notify.sh`\\n    77\\tentries. Multiple hooks for one event run concurrently, so hook commands must be\\n    78\\tidempotent and must not rely on registration order.\\n    79\\t\\n    80\\t### 3.1 Tool-Level Guard Adapters (Claude / OpenCode parity)\\n    81\\t\\n    82\\tBeyond the session-lifecycle hooks, Codex mirrors the repo's guard hooks as thin\\n    83\\tadapters over the same runtime-neutral cores the Claude hooks and OpenCode plugins\\n    84\\tuse. Each fails open (exit 0 on empty/malformed stdin); the two deny-capable guards\\n    85\\temit `hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision: \\\"deny\\\"`, which Codex honors.\\n    86\\t\\n    87\\t| Event · matcher | Guard | Adapter |\\n    88\\t|---|---|---|\\n    89\\t| PreToolUse · `exec\\\\|Bash\\\\|apply_patch\\\\|edit` | spec-gate enforce (deny-capable) | `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/codex/spec-gate-enforce.mjs` |\\n    90\\t| UserPromptSubmit | spec-gate classify (state only, no emit) | `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/codex/spec-gate-classify.mjs` |\\n    91\\t| PreToolUse · `exec\\\\|Bash` | dispatch preflight lint (deny-capable) | `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-preflight-lint.mjs` |\\n    92\\t| PostToolUse · `apply_patch\\\\|edit` | post-edit quality | `.skilled/hooks/post-edit-quality/codex/post-edit-quality.cjs` |\\n    93\\t| PostToolUse · `exec\\\\|Bash` | dispatch audit (observe) | `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/codex/dispatch-audit-posttooluse.mjs` |\\n    94\\t| Stop | completion-evidence sentinel (advisory) | `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/codex/completion-evidence-stop.cjs` |\\n    95\\t| PreToolUse · `mcp__.*` | mcp route guard (dormant until an external MCP family registers) | `.skilled/hooks/mcp-route-guard/codex/mcp-route-guard.cjs` |\\n    96\\t\\n    97\\t> Codex delivers an `apply_patch` target path inside `tool_input.command` (the\\n    98\\t> `*** Add/Update/Delete File:` header), not a `file_path` field — filePath-driven\\n    99\\t> adapters parse the path from the patch body. A Stop hook's stdout is parsed as a\\n   100\\t> response envelope, so neutral shell scripts wired to Stop must not emit stdout.\\n   101\\t\\n   102\\t> Codex 0.160 renamed its shell tool from `exec` to `Bash`, and a matcher of\\n   103\\t> `exec` alone no longer fires for a shell call. Every shell matcher names both.\\n   104\\t> The same release hands PostToolUse the shell output as one string where older\\n   105\\t> builds handed `{stdout, stderr}`, so the audit adapter reads either shape.\\n   106\\t\\n   107\\t> Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\\n   108\\t> The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\\\"...\\\"]` as a\\n   109\\t> `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\\n   110\\t> the approval is a hash, a changed entry is likely to need approving again. After a\\n   111\\t> `hooks.json` change, open `/hooks` in an interactive Codex session and approve every\\n   112\\t> entry it lists as needing review.\\n   113\\t\\n   114\\t> Codex has no task-dispatch guard. Its `spawn_agent` call reaches PreToolUse as\\n   115\\t> `collaborationspawn_agent`, but the hook payload carries the spawn `message`\\n   116\\t> encrypted (`gAAAAA...`) beside `task_name` and `fork_turns`, so the guard has no\\n   117\\t> prompt or target to check.\\n   118\\t\\n   119\\tCodex loads the full repo hook set (lifecycle and guards) from `.codex/hooks.json`.\\n   120\\tThe user-global `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of it, or every hook runs\\n   121\\ttwice. `.skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` removes such copies, backs up first,\\n   122\\tand keeps Superset and other third-party entries.\\n   123\\t\\n   124\\t---\\n   125\\t\\n   126\\t## 4. EVENT INPUTS\\n   127\\t\\n   128\\tHook commands receive JSON on stdin. Current generated schemas use snake_case\\n   129\\tfield names and reject unknown properties.\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t`SessionStart` required shape:\\n   132\\t\\n   133\\t```json\\n   134\\t{\\n   135\\t  \\\"session_id\\\": \\\"uuid\\\",\\n\"}],\"providerCall\":{\"executed\":false},\"providerResult\":{\"executed\":false},\"rawInput\":\"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/315-frontend-reference-restructure && git show HEAD:.skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/references/hook-contract.md | sed -n '60,135p' | nl -ba -v60\\\"}\"},\"time\":{\"start\":1793796371087,\"end\":1793796371522}}}}\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/review-1.md.json:120:{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"timestamp\":1793796528657,\"sessionID\":\"ses_e596b6c59ffeYFKlEpeH4Zk8ze\",\"part\":{\"partID\":\"prt_1a69ad5f9002wlKbgB6xjM7JAE\",\"sessionID\":\"ses_e596b6c59ffeYFKlEpeH4Zk8ze\",\"messageID\":\"msg_1a69ac677001YY0nFSwG6nY6rX\",\"type\":\"tool\",\"id\":\"call_00_pzzzqvu2qkmw5umagctuoj47\",\"tool\":\"shell\",\"state\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/315-frontend-reference-restructure && for f in ROUTER.md SKILL.md assets/prompt-quality-card.md assets/prompt-templates.md references/agent-delegation.md references/cli-reference.md references/codex-tools.md references/hook-contract.md references/providers-and-models.md; do echo \\\"================== $f\\\"; git diff --word-diff=porcelain -U0 -- \\\".skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/$f\\\" | grep -E '^[-+][^-+]' | sed 's/^\\\\([-+]\\\\)/\\\\1 /' | head -120; done\"},\"output\":\"================== ROUTER.md\\n- are\\n+ are:\\n- `references/` —\\n+ `references/`:\\n- roster — and\\n+ roster;\\n+ | ON_DEMAND_KEYWORDS | When a listed phrase appears | The extended reference for that phrase |\\n================== SKILL.md\\n+ Keep every path inside this skill folder, and load a file only when it exists.\\n+ When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the checklist from the machine router instead of guessing.\\n- that\\n- dispatch,\\n+ dispatch\\n- result, and must be honored\\n+ result. Honor them\\n- 9\\n+ 8\\n- proper anchor comments.\\n+ integer numbering.\\n================== assets/prompt-quality-card.md\\n- `../../../sk-prompt/references/patterns-evaluation.md`)\\n+ [patterns-evaluation.md](../../../sk-prompt/references/patterns-evaluation.md))\\n- today.\\n+ are recorded here.\\n- only `gpt-5.5`;\\n+ `gpt-5.5` by default;\\n- `../../../sk-prompt/assets/cli-prompt-quality-card.md`\\n+ [cli-prompt-quality-card.md](../../../sk-prompt/assets/cli-prompt-quality-card.md)\\n- `../SKILL.md`.\\n+ [SKILL.md](../SKILL.md).\\n- 3\\n+ 2\\n- `./prompt-templates.md`\\n+ [prompt-templates.md](./prompt-templates.md)\\n- `../SKILL.md`\\n+ [SKILL.md](../SKILL.md)\\n================== assets/prompt-templates.md\\n- browsing during execution\\n+ browsing. A top-level flag, so it precedes the subcommand: `codex --search exec …`.\\n+ | `[files]`          | Files to stage before a review               | `src/auth.ts`, `src/session.ts`                                |\\n+ | `[feature]`        | Feature the task builds                     | `\\\"OAuth2 PKCE flow\\\"`, `\\\"rate limiter\\\"`                         |\\n+ | `[test-file]`      | Existing test file to compare against the implementation | `src/utils/validator.test.ts`                     |\\n+ | `[source-file]`    | Implementation file the tests cover         | `src/utils/validator.ts`                                       |\\n+ | `[approach]`       | Named pattern to apply                      | `\\\"repository\\\"`, `\\\"sliding-window\\\"`                              |\\n- RELATED RESOURCES\\n+ REASONING EFFORT SELECTION\\n- ### Parent\\n+ All templates dispatch `--model gpt-5.5`. Tune `model_reasoning_effort` to the task.\\n+ ### High Reasoning (architecture, security, planning)\\n- ### References\\n+ Raise effort to `high` or `xhigh` for tasks that benefit from deeper analysis:\\n+ ```bash\\n+ # Architecture analysis\\n+ codex exec \\\"Analyze the architecture of this project. Identify coupling issues, circular dependencies,\\n- flag reference\\n+ scalability concerns.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n+ # Security audit\\n+ codex exec \\\"Perform a thorough security audit of @./[file]. Check OWASP Top 10, auth bypasses, injection vectors, and cryptographic weaknesses.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n+ # Complex planning\\n+ codex exec -p ai-council \\\"Design 3 alternative caching strategies for this API. Score each on correctness, maintainability, and performance.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"xhigh\\\" -s read-only\\n+ ```\\n+ ### Medium Reasoning (default: code, review, docs)\\n+ `medium` is the skill default. Works for code generation, standard review, implementation, and documentation:\\n+ ```bash\\n+ # Code generation\\n+ codex exec \\\"Create a [description] in [language]. Requirements: [requirements]. Output complete file.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n+ # Standard code review\\n+ codex exec \\\"Review @./[file] for bugs, style issues, and edge cases.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n+ # Test generation\\n+ codex exec \\\"Generate comprehensive unit tests for @./[file].\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n+ ```\\n+ ### Mixed Effort Workflow\\n+ Use `high` for analysis, then `medium` for implementation, the same model at a different depth:\\n+ ```bash\\n+ # Step 1: Deep analysis at high reasoning\\n+ codex exec \\\"Identify all security vulnerabilities in @./src/auth/\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only > /tmp/security-findings.txt\\n+ # Step 2: Fix at medium reasoning\\n+ codex exec \\\"Fix these security issues: $(cat /tmp/security-findings.txt)\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n+ ```\\n- REASONING EFFORT SELECTION\\n+ RELATED RESOURCES\\n- All templates dispatch `--model gpt-5.5`. Tune `model_reasoning_effort` to the task.\\n- High Reasoning (architecture, security, planning)\\n- Raise effort to `high` or `xhigh` for tasks that benefit from deeper analysis:\\n- ```bash\\n- # Architecture analysis\\n- codex exec \\\"Analyze the architecture of this project. Identify coupling issues, circular dependencies, and scalability concerns.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n- # Security audit\\n- codex exec \\\"Perform a thorough security audit of @./[file]. Check OWASP Top 10, auth bypasses, injection vectors, and cryptographic weaknesses.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n- # Complex planning\\n- codex exec -p ai-council \\\"Design 3 alternative caching strategies for this API. Score each on correctness, maintainability, and performance.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"xhigh\\\" -s read-only\\n- ```\\n- ### Medium Reasoning (default: code, review, docs)\\n- `medium` is the skill default. Works for code generation, standard review, implementation, and documentation:\\n- ```bash\\n- # Code generation\\n- codex exec \\\"Create a [description] in [language]. Requirements: [requirements]. Output complete file.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n- # Standard code review\\n- codex exec \\\"Review @./[file] for bugs, style issues, and edge cases.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n- # Test generation\\n- codex exec \\\"Generate comprehensive unit tests for @./[file].\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n- ```\\n- ### Mixed Effort Workflow\\n+ Parent\\n- Use `high` for analysis, then `medium` for implementation, the same model at a different depth:\\n- ```bash\\n- # Step 1: Deep analysis at high reasoning\\n- codex exec \\\"Identify all security vulnerabilities in @./src/auth/\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only > /tmp/security-findings.txt\\n+ ### References\\n- # Step 2: Fix at medium reasoning\\n- codex exec \\\"Fix these security issues: $(cat /tmp/security-findings.txt)\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n- ```\\n================== references/agent-delegation.md\\n- Routing reference for delegating tasks to specialized\\n+ Open it when a task needs a\\n- CLI agents.\\n+ agent with its own sandbox and reasoning settings rather than a plain prompt.\\n- ### Core Principle\\n- The calling AI decides WHAT to do, Codex CLI decides HOW to do it within the delegated scope.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ The calling AI decides WHAT to do, Codex CLI decides HOW to do it within the delegated scope.\\n================== references/cli-reference.md\\n- Comprehensive reference for all Codex CLI commands, flags, models, configuration,\\n+ Open it when a dispatch has to be exact,\\n- troubleshooting.\\n+ look up the flag, the profile path or the sandbox mode instead of guessing.\\n- ### Core Principle\\n- Codex CLI is OpenAI's terminal-based AI coding agent powered by `gpt-5.5` — a balanced model used across code generation, review, architecture analysis, and research. The skill dispatches `gpt-5.5` at `medium` reasoning on the `fast` service tier by default; users can override reasoning effort (e.g. \\\"Use gpt 5.5 high fast\\\"). It provides direct access to OpenAI's capabilities from the command line, including code generation, file manipulation, shell execution, web browsing, and multi-turn sessions — all governed by configurable sandbox modes.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ Codex CLI is OpenAI's terminal-based AI coding agent powered by `gpt-5.5`, a balanced model used across code generation, review, architecture analysis, and research. The skill dispatches `gpt-5.5` at `medium` reasoning on the `fast` service tier by default; users can override reasoning effort (e.g. \\\"Use gpt 5.5 high fast\\\"). It provides direct access to OpenAI's capabilities from the command line, including code generation, file manipulation, shell execution, web browsing, and multi-turn sessions, all governed by configurable sandbox modes.\\n- https://github.com/openai/codex\\n+ [openai/codex](https://github.com/openai/codex)\\n================== references/codex-tools.md\\n- Reference for all Codex CLI capabilities, highlighting unique features\\n+ Open it when a task turns on a Codex-only surface, such as a diff-aware review or a live web lookup,\\n- Claude Code equivalents.\\n+ you need the exact invocation.\\n- ### Core Principle\\n- Delegate to Codex CLI for capabilities Claude Code lacks natively — especially `/review` for diff-aware code review workflows, `--search` for live web browsing, and session management (resume, fork) for multi-turn task continuity.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ Delegate to Codex CLI for capabilities Claude Code lacks natively, especially `/review` for diff-aware code review workflows, `--search` for live web browsing, and session management (resume, fork) for multi-turn task continuity.\\n================== references/hook-contract.md\\n+ Open this before wiring a Codex hook or debugging one whose output never reaches the model. It pins the activation flag, registration shape, stdin and stdout contracts, and fail-open exit behavior.\\n+ ### Purpose\\n+ Spec Kit uses the native `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` surfaces to inject startup context and the compact skill-advisor brief.\\n+ ### When to Use\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n- Spec Kit uses the native `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` surfaces\\n- to inject startup context and the compact skill-advisor brief.\\n+ > Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\\n+ > The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\\\"...\\\"]` as a\\n+ > `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\\n+ > the approval is a hash, a changed entry is likely to need approving again. After a\\n+ > `hooks.json` change, open `/hooks` in an interactive Codex session and approve every\\n+ > entry it lists as needing review.\\n+ Codex loads the full repo hook set (lifecycle and guards) from `.codex/hooks.json`.\\n+ The user-global `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of it, or every hook runs\\n+ twice. `.skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` removes such copies, backs up first,\\n+ and keeps Superset and other third-party entries.\\n- > Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\\n- > The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\\\"...\\\"]` as a\\n- > `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\\n- > the approval is a hash, a changed entry is likely to need approving again. After a\\n- > `hooks.json` change, open `/hooks` in an interactive Codex session and approve every\\n- > entry it lists as needing review.\\n- Codex loads the full repo hook set (lifecycle and guards) from `.codex/hooks.json`.\\n- The user-global `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of it, or every hook runs\\n- twice. `.skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` removes such copies, backs up first,\\n- and keeps Superset and other third-party entries.\\n- For `SessionStart`,\\n- is\\n- placed\\n- for `UserPromptSubmit`,\\n+ `UserPromptSubmit` puts\\n- is placed\\n-   `<spec-folder>`\\n================== references/providers-and-models.md\\n- Core Principle\\n+ Purpose\\n- This mode is single-provider (OpenAI via ChatGPT OAuth) — the choice space is one provider, five models, and one effort ladder capped per model.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ This mode is single-provider (OpenAI via ChatGPT OAuth); the choice space is one provider, five models, and one effort ladder capped per model.\\n- §6),\\n+ [../SKILL.md](../SKILL.md) §4 Rule 10),\\n- default\\n- medium`; raise\\n+ medium`.\\n- planning; escalate\\n+ planning.\\n- `xhigh` —\\n+ `xhigh`:\\n- review; drop\\n+ review.\\n- `plan_mode_reasoning_effort` —\\n+ `plan_mode_reasoning_effort`;\\n+ `codex exec` defaults to `--sandbox read-only`, so file-modification tasks silently no-op unless you add `--sandbox workspace-write` (or `--full-auto`). See [integration-patterns.md](./integration-patterns.md).\\n- `codex exec` defaults to `--sandbox read-only`, so file-modification tasks silently no-op unless you add `--sandbox workspace-write` (or `--full-auto`). See [integration-patterns.md](./integration-patterns.md).\\n\",\"title\":\"shell\",\"metadata\":{\"metadata\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit\":0},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"================== ROUTER.md\\n- are\\n+ are:\\n- `references/` —\\n+ `references/`:\\n- roster — and\\n+ roster;\\n+ | ON_DEMAND_KEYWORDS | When a listed phrase appears | The extended reference for that phrase |\\n================== SKILL.md\\n+ Keep every path inside this skill folder, and load a file only when it exists.\\n+ When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the checklist from the machine router instead of guessing.\\n- that\\n- dispatch,\\n+ dispatch\\n- result, and must be honored\\n+ result. Honor them\\n- 9\\n+ 8\\n- proper anchor comments.\\n+ integer numbering.\\n================== assets/prompt-quality-card.md\\n- `../../../sk-prompt/references/patterns-evaluation.md`)\\n+ [patterns-evaluation.md](../../../sk-prompt/references/patterns-evaluation.md))\\n- today.\\n+ are recorded here.\\n- only `gpt-5.5`;\\n+ `gpt-5.5` by default;\\n- `../../../sk-prompt/assets/cli-prompt-quality-card.md`\\n+ [cli-prompt-quality-card.md](../../../sk-prompt/assets/cli-prompt-quality-card.md)\\n- `../SKILL.md`.\\n+ [SKILL.md](../SKILL.md).\\n- 3\\n+ 2\\n- `./prompt-templates.md`\\n+ [prompt-templates.md](./prompt-templates.md)\\n- `../SKILL.md`\\n+ [SKILL.md](../SKILL.md)\\n================== assets/prompt-templates.md\\n- browsing during execution\\n+ browsing. A top-level flag, so it precedes the subcommand: `codex --search exec …`.\\n+ | `[files]`          | Files to stage before a review               | `src/auth.ts`, `src/session.ts`                                |\\n+ | `[feature]`        | Feature the task builds                     | `\\\"OAuth2 PKCE flow\\\"`, `\\\"rate limiter\\\"`                         |\\n+ | `[test-file]`      | Existing test file to compare against the implementation | `src/utils/validator.test.ts`                     |\\n+ | `[source-file]`    | Implementation file the tests cover         | `src/utils/validator.ts`                                       |\\n+ | `[approach]`       | Named pattern to apply                      | `\\\"repository\\\"`, `\\\"sliding-window\\\"`                              |\\n- RELATED RESOURCES\\n+ REASONING EFFORT SELECTION\\n- ### Parent\\n+ All templates dispatch `--model gpt-5.5`. Tune `model_reasoning_effort` to the task.\\n+ ### High Reasoning (architecture, security, planning)\\n- ### References\\n+ Raise effort to `high` or `xhigh` for tasks that benefit from deeper analysis:\\n+ ```bash\\n+ # Architecture analysis\\n+ codex exec \\\"Analyze the architecture of this project. Identify coupling issues, circular dependencies,\\n- flag reference\\n+ scalability concerns.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n+ # Security audit\\n+ codex exec \\\"Perform a thorough security audit of @./[file]. Check OWASP Top 10, auth bypasses, injection vectors, and cryptographic weaknesses.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n+ # Complex planning\\n+ codex exec -p ai-council \\\"Design 3 alternative caching strategies for this API. Score each on correctness, maintainability, and performance.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"xhigh\\\" -s read-only\\n+ ```\\n+ ### Medium Reasoning (default: code, review, docs)\\n+ `medium` is the skill default. Works for code generation, standard review, implementation, and documentation:\\n+ ```bash\\n+ # Code generation\\n+ codex exec \\\"Create a [description] in [language]. Requirements: [requirements]. Output complete file.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n+ # Standard code review\\n+ codex exec \\\"Review @./[file] for bugs, style issues, and edge cases.\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n+ # Test generation\\n+ codex exec \\\"Generate comprehensive unit tests for @./[file].\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n+ ```\\n+ ### Mixed Effort Workflow\\n+ Use `high` for analysis, then `medium` for implementation, the same model at a different depth:\\n+ ```bash\\n+ # Step 1: Deep analysis at high reasoning\\n+ codex exec \\\"Identify all security vulnerabilities in @./src/auth/\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only > /tmp/security-findings.txt\\n+ # Step 2: Fix at medium reasoning\\n+ codex exec \\\"Fix these security issues: $(cat /tmp/security-findings.txt)\\\" \\\\\\n+   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n+ ```\\n- REASONING EFFORT SELECTION\\n+ RELATED RESOURCES\\n- All templates dispatch `--model gpt-5.5`. Tune `model_reasoning_effort` to the task.\\n- High Reasoning (architecture, security, planning)\\n- Raise effort to `high` or `xhigh` for tasks that benefit from deeper analysis:\\n- ```bash\\n- # Architecture analysis\\n- codex exec \\\"Analyze the architecture of this project. Identify coupling issues, circular dependencies, and scalability concerns.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n- # Security audit\\n- codex exec \\\"Perform a thorough security audit of @./[file]. Check OWASP Top 10, auth bypasses, injection vectors, and cryptographic weaknesses.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n- # Complex planning\\n- codex exec -p ai-council \\\"Design 3 alternative caching strategies for this API. Score each on correctness, maintainability, and performance.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"xhigh\\\" -s read-only\\n- ```\\n- ### Medium Reasoning (default: code, review, docs)\\n- `medium` is the skill default. Works for code generation, standard review, implementation, and documentation:\\n- ```bash\\n- # Code generation\\n- codex exec \\\"Create a [description] in [language]. Requirements: [requirements]. Output complete file.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n- # Standard code review\\n- codex exec \\\"Review @./[file] for bugs, style issues, and edge cases.\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox read-only\\n- # Test generation\\n- codex exec \\\"Generate comprehensive unit tests for @./[file].\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n- ```\\n- ### Mixed Effort Workflow\\n+ Parent\\n- Use `high` for analysis, then `medium` for implementation, the same model at a different depth:\\n- ```bash\\n- # Step 1: Deep analysis at high reasoning\\n- codex exec \\\"Identify all security vulnerabilities in @./src/auth/\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"high\\\" --sandbox read-only > /tmp/security-findings.txt\\n+ ### References\\n- # Step 2: Fix at medium reasoning\\n- codex exec \\\"Fix these security issues: $(cat /tmp/security-findings.txt)\\\" \\\\\\n-   --model gpt-5.5 -c model_reasoning_effort=\\\"medium\\\" --sandbox workspace-write\\n- ```\\n================== references/agent-delegation.md\\n- Routing reference for delegating tasks to specialized\\n+ Open it when a task needs a\\n- CLI agents.\\n+ agent with its own sandbox and reasoning settings rather than a plain prompt.\\n- ### Core Principle\\n- The calling AI decides WHAT to do, Codex CLI decides HOW to do it within the delegated scope.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ The calling AI decides WHAT to do, Codex CLI decides HOW to do it within the delegated scope.\\n================== references/cli-reference.md\\n- Comprehensive reference for all Codex CLI commands, flags, models, configuration,\\n+ Open it when a dispatch has to be exact,\\n- troubleshooting.\\n+ look up the flag, the profile path or the sandbox mode instead of guessing.\\n- ### Core Principle\\n- Codex CLI is OpenAI's terminal-based AI coding agent powered by `gpt-5.5` — a balanced model used across code generation, review, architecture analysis, and research. The skill dispatches `gpt-5.5` at `medium` reasoning on the `fast` service tier by default; users can override reasoning effort (e.g. \\\"Use gpt 5.5 high fast\\\"). It provides direct access to OpenAI's capabilities from the command line, including code generation, file manipulation, shell execution, web browsing, and multi-turn sessions — all governed by configurable sandbox modes.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ Codex CLI is OpenAI's terminal-based AI coding agent powered by `gpt-5.5`, a balanced model used across code generation, review, architecture analysis, and research. The skill dispatches `gpt-5.5` at `medium` reasoning on the `fast` service tier by default; users can override reasoning effort (e.g. \\\"Use gpt 5.5 high fast\\\"). It provides direct access to OpenAI's capabilities from the command line, including code generation, file manipulation, shell execution, web browsing, and multi-turn sessions, all governed by configurable sandbox modes.\\n- https://github.com/openai/codex\\n+ [openai/codex](https://github.com/openai/codex)\\n================== references/codex-tools.md\\n- Reference for all Codex CLI capabilities, highlighting unique features\\n+ Open it when a task turns on a Codex-only surface, such as a diff-aware review or a live web lookup,\\n- Claude Code equivalents.\\n+ you need the exact invocation.\\n- ### Core Principle\\n- Delegate to Codex CLI for capabilities Claude Code lacks natively — especially `/review` for diff-aware code review workflows, `--search` for live web browsing, and session management (resume, fork) for multi-turn task continuity.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ Delegate to Codex CLI for capabilities Claude Code lacks natively, especially `/review` for diff-aware code review workflows, `--search` for live web browsing, and session management (resume, fork) for multi-turn task continuity.\\n================== references/hook-contract.md\\n+ Open this before wiring a Codex hook or debugging one whose output never reaches the model. It pins the activation flag, registration shape, stdin and stdout contracts, and fail-open exit behavior.\\n+ ### Purpose\\n+ Spec Kit uses the native `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` surfaces to inject startup context and the compact skill-advisor brief.\\n+ ### When to Use\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n- Spec Kit uses the native `SessionStart` and `UserPromptSubmit` surfaces\\n- to inject startup context and the compact skill-advisor brief.\\n+ > Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\\n+ > The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\\\"...\\\"]` as a\\n+ > `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\\n+ > the approval is a hash, a changed entry is likely to need approving again. After a\\n+ > `hooks.json` change, open `/hooks` in an interactive Codex session and approve every\\n+ > entry it lists as needing review.\\n+ Codex loads the full repo hook set (lifecycle and guards) from `.codex/hooks.json`.\\n+ The user-global `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of it, or every hook runs\\n+ twice. `.skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` removes such copies, backs up first,\\n+ and keeps Superset and other third-party entries.\\n- > Codex runs a project hook only after the operator approves that exact entry.\\n- > The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\\\"...\\\"]` as a\\n- > `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\\n- > the approval is a hash, a changed entry is likely to need approving again. After a\\n- > `hooks.json` change, open `/hooks` in an interactive Codex session and approve every\\n- > entry it lists as needing review.\\n- Codex loads the full repo hook set (lifecycle and guards) from `.codex/hooks.json`.\\n- The user-global `~/.codex/hooks.json` must hold no copy of it, or every hook runs\\n- twice. `.skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs` removes such copies, backs up first,\\n- and keeps Superset and other third-party entries.\\n- For `SessionStart`,\\n- is\\n- placed\\n- for `UserPromptSubmit`,\\n+ `UserPromptSubmit` puts\\n- is placed\\n-   `<spec-folder>`\\n================== references/providers-and-models.md\\n- Core Principle\\n+ Purpose\\n- This mode is single-provider (OpenAI via ChatGPT OAuth) — the choice space is one provider, five models, and one effort ladder capped per model.\\n+ ### Core Principle\\n+ This mode is single-provider (OpenAI via ChatGPT OAuth); the choice space is one provider, five models, and one effort ladder capped per model.\\n- §6),\\n+ [../SKILL.md](../SKILL.md) §4 Rule 10),\\n- default\\n- medium`; raise\\n+ medium`.\\n- planning; escalate\\n+ planning.\\n- `xhigh` —\\n+ `xhigh`:\\n- review; drop\\n+ review.\\n- `plan_mode_reasoning_effort` —\\n+ `plan_mode_reasoning_effort`;\\n+ `codex exec` defaults to `--sandbox read-only`, so file-modification tasks silently no-op unless you add `--sandbox workspace-write` (or `--full-auto`). See [integration-patterns.md](./integration-patterns.md).\\n- `codex exec` defaults to `--sandbox read-only`, so file-modification tasks silently no-op unless you add `--sandbox workspace-write` (or `--full-auto`). See [integration-patterns.md](./integration-patterns.md).\\n\"}],\"providerCall\":{\"executed\":false},\"providerResult\":{\"executed\":false},\"rawInput\":\"{\\\"command\\\": \\\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/315-frontend-reference-restructure && for f in ROUTER.md SKILL.md assets/prompt-quality-card.md assets/prompt-templates.md references/agent-delegation.md references/cli-reference.md references/codex-tools.md references/hook-contract.md references/providers-and-models.md; do echo \\\\\\\"================== $f\\\\\\\"; git diff --word-diff=porcelain -U0 -- \\\\\\\".skilled/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/$f\\\\\\\" | grep -E '^[-+][^-+]' | sed 's/^\\\\\\\\([-+]\\\\\\\\)/\\\\\\\\1 /' | head -120; done\\\"}\"},\"time\":{\"start\":1793796527609,\"end\":1793796528657}}}}\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:475:+> The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\"...\"]` as a\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:476:+> `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:494:-> The approval is stored in `~/.codex/config.toml` under `[hooks.state.\"...\"]` as a\n./specs/sk-doc/authoring-modes-and-playbooks/005-skill-readability-refinement/002-cli-external-orchestration/003-cli-codex/scratch/diff-1.patch:495:-> `trusted_hash`, and an entry with no approval is skipped without a warning. Because\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/deep-research-strategy.md:46:- q6-external-refs: **Four live `.opencode`-naming references outside the repo, plus a surviving path-keyed class.** Live: `~/.config/git/hooks/*` (7 symlinks → main checkout), `~/.codex/hooks.json` (18 command strings), `~/.hermes/config.yaml` MCP args, `~/.codex/config.toml` `[projects.\"…/Public/.opencode\"]` trust entry. Survives: registries keyed on the checkout root (`~/.codex` projects + hooks.state, `~/.hermes` trusted_project_dirs, `~/.claude.json`), `~/.pi/agent/*` → `.pi/*` symlinks, `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. Clean negatives named. No in-repo validator checks this class. (iteration 4)\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-004.md:73:- `~/.codex/config.toml` `[projects.\"…/Public\"]` trust entry and all `[hooks.state.\"…/.codex/hooks.json:…\"]` keys [SOURCE: ~/.codex/config.toml:15,115-417]\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/research.md:72:Four live `.opencode`-naming references, none of which moves with `git mv`: the seven git-hook symlinks; `~/.codex/hooks.json` (18 `.opencode` command strings, installed outbound by `install-codex-hooks.mjs`); `~/.hermes/config.yaml`'s `mcp_servers.code_mode` args; and `~/.codex/config.toml`'s `[projects.\"…/Public/.opencode\"]` trust entry [SOURCE: iteration-004.md F4.7]. The surviving class keys on the checkout root instead: `~/.codex` `[projects.\"…/Public\"]` and `[hooks.state.*]`, `~/.hermes` `trusted_project_dirs`, `~/.claude.json`, `~/.pi/agent/*` → `.pi/*` symlinks, `~/.codex/AGENTS.md`. Clean negatives were checked and named (`~/.claude/settings.json`, `~/.config/devin/*`, `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `~/.claude/plugins/`); `~/.zshrc`'s `$HOME/.opencode/bin` entry is stale (directory absent). **No in-repo validator covers this class** — the inventory is the whole safety net [SOURCE: iteration-004.md F4.8].\n./specs/system-speckit/runtime-and-release/001-system-speckit-v4/041-skilled-source-root-migration/001-deep-research/research/lineages/deepseek/findings-registry.json:8:    {\"id\": \"q6-external-refs\", \"text\": \"Which references live outside the repository and will silently keep pointing at the old path?\", \"resolvedAt\": \"iteration-4\", \"answer\": \"Four live .opencode-naming references: 7 git-hook symlinks (~/.config/git/hooks → main checkout), ~/.codex/hooks.json (18 command strings), ~/.hermes/config.yaml mcp_servers args, ~/.codex/config.toml [projects.\\\".../Public/.opencode\\\"] trust entry. Survives (checkout-root-keyed): ~/.codex projects + hooks.state, ~/.hermes trusted_project_dirs, ~/.claude.json, ~/.pi/agent/* → .pi/* symlinks, ~/.codex/AGENTS.md. Clean negatives: ~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.config/devin/*, ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, ~/.claude/plugins. ~/.zshrc $HOME/.opencode/bin entry is stale (dir absent). No in-repo validator covers this class.\"},\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/codex-hooks-client.mjs.txt:3://   node codex-hooks.mjs trust <cwd> <key>...  writes hooks.state.\"<key>\".trusted_hash = the hash Codex reports now,\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/codex-hooks-client.mjs.txt:54:      return { keyPath: `hooks.state.\"${key}\".trusted_hash`, mergeStrategy: 'upsert', value: h.currentHash };\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:4:< trusted_hash = \"sha256:024cc055c67890d2d436364490bacaf8bee2c8224efdf66add12889091efd082\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:6:> trusted_hash = \"sha256:0514fa8861fa752349708dd1d0ee0167c9333984d1f2bf93b3c8e5fb5be0b2aa\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:8:< trusted_hash = \"sha256:0514fa8861fa752349708dd1d0ee0167c9333984d1f2bf93b3c8e5fb5be0b2aa\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:10:> trusted_hash = \"sha256:b9674f1ef82659aecbd346ee56c3c6d6fc9327df619774917e78c0b2b134ab64\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:12:> [hooks.state.\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/.codex/hooks.json:session_start:0:1\"]\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/010-review-advisories-and-codex-cleanup/evidence/c1/config-trust-diff.txt:13:> trusted_hash = \"sha256:87545efe882d91c73e06a6216984f32c339e4cc5f1bc2d5c3039146246b2aca5\"\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/host-integrations/003-pi-skill-orchestrator-based-research-refinement/009-test-findings-remediation/implementation-summary.md:227:   Codex keys hook trust by position: `~/.codex/config.toml` holds a `trusted_hash` for each `<file>:<event>:<group>:<hook>`. Deleting the first SessionStart group moved every later one up a place, so Codex now treats the global SessionStart hooks as new or changed and skips them. A plain `codex exec` from the checkout started 6 SessionStart hooks, the project file's count, where 16 ran before. The installer moves none of the hooks it keeps. Run it first, then start `codex` once in a terminal and trust the hooks its startup review lists, or trust them later with `/hooks`. Expect three: nodeterm, GitKraken and orca. Trusting before the installer run would also re-trust the six SessionStart copies, and every session would run them twice again until the installer removes them. To undo the jcode deletion instead, copy the backup back over `~/.codex/hooks.json`.\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/spec.md:99:$ rg -n \"trusted_hash\" .codex/README.md\n./specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/010-reachability-and-attribution/spec.md:100:54:2. Approve the hooks. Codex runs a project hook only after you approve that exact entry. Open `/hooks` in an interactive session and approve each entry it lists for review. The approval is stored as a `trusted_hash` in `~/.codex/config.toml`, so a changed entry needs approving again.\n\n[showing lines 20-40 of 40; full output saved to /Users/michelkerkmeester/.local/share/opencode/shell/4c10847297631c3af43d766cf1908920fe00c449/sh_1d22a3b7b001SAdr4X8qAzfn4H.out]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"rg -n \\\"trusted_hash|hooks\\\\.state\\\" --glob '!**/node_modules/**' --glob '!**/dist/**' -g '!*.jsonl' . 2>/dev/null | head -40\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527344197,"end":1794527350204}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794527353772,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd82e01ffeqmnJRIxBdI7fut","part":{"id":"prt_1d22a5bac001rLXZgmS931E7zv","sessionID":"ses_e2dd82e01ffeqmnJRIxBdI7fut","messageID":"msg_1d229f961001ayrFpUVBDlHsL9","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"8de25b2cf7b2073e4b6d92f6880b8cba1a9753e5","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":23407,"output":236,"reasoning":114,"cache":{"read":43392,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794527359696,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd82e01ffeqmnJRIxBdI7fut","part":{"id":"prt_1d22a72d0001YmYkHwwQZr9PBU","sessionID":"ses_e2dd82e01ffeqmnJRIxBdI7fut","messageID":"msg_1d22a5bb600194a3lp8tOPbE0k","type":"step-start","snapshot":"b99101f1d704bcb6c7a4606675d7d8deda5cc46c"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527379866,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd82e01ffeqmnJRIxBdI7fut","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22abfaa0018EBrN6ktLR0z5z","sessionID":"ses_e2dd82e01ffeqmnJRIxBdI7fut","messageID":"msg_1d22a5bb600194a3lp8tOPbE0k","type":"tool","id":"call_c440e51156fe4a8fa0925957","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '1,60p' .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"---\ntitle: \"Doctor Command Scripts\"\ndescription: \"Developer reference for doctor routing, MCP diagnostics, release updates and structural integrity checks.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"doctor command scripts\"\n  - \"doctor route validation\"\n  - \"mcp diagnostics\"\nimportance_tier: \"important\"\n---\n\n# Doctor Command Scripts\n\n> Documents `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/`. This README uses the `.markdown` extension because\n> OpenCode registers every `.md` file under `.skilled/commands/` as a command.\n>\n> Diagnostic and validation entrypoints used by the doctor command family.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW AND PURPOSE\n\n`.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/` contains shell, JavaScript and Python tools that support doctor routes.\n\nThe scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent skill hubs, report graph freshness, check a skill's distance from the Skilled format and convert one, read the root instruction file against the shipped template and embed or restore it, then plan and apply framework release updates. Most are read-only. The release updater's apply and rollback paths write local state, and so does the skill converter's `--apply`. Every script has a test under `tests/`, except `skill-graph-freshness.cjs`, whose freshness panel test lives in the skill advisor runtime. One runner executes them all.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DIRECTORY TREE\n\n```text\n.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/\n+-- agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs\n+-- agents-md-check.cjs\n+-- agents-md-embed.cjs\n+-- agents-md-manifest.cjs\n+-- audit_descriptions.py\n+-- command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n+-- check-mcp-mutation-class.sh\n+-- ci-report.cjs\n+-- ci-scaffold.cjs\n+-- ci-toggle.cjs\n+-- doctor-helper-match.cjs\n+-- doctor-phrase-reach.cjs\n+-- git-hook-gates.cjs\n+-- git-setup-check.cjs\n+-- git-standards.cjs\n+-- mcp-doctor-lib.sh\n+-- mcp-doctor.sh\n+-- opencode-version-battery.cjs\n+-- orphan-process-check.cjs\n+-- parent-skill-check.cjs\n+-- route-validate.py\n+-- route-validate.sh\n+-- release-update.cjs\n+-- sk-code-quality-config.cjs\n+-- sk-code-setup-check.cjs\n+-- sk-skill-check.cjs\n+-- sk-skill-convert.cjs\n+-- skill-graph-freshness.cjs\n+-- lib/\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ntitle: \"Doctor Command Scripts\"\ndescription: \"Developer reference for doctor routing, MCP diagnostics, release updates and structural integrity checks.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"doctor command scripts\"\n  - \"doctor route validation\"\n  - \"mcp diagnostics\"\nimportance_tier: \"important\"\n---\n\n# Doctor Command Scripts\n\n> Documents `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/`. This README uses the `.markdown` extension because\n> OpenCode registers every `.md` file under `.skilled/commands/` as a command.\n>\n> Diagnostic and validation entrypoints used by the doctor command family.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW AND PURPOSE\n\n`.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/` contains shell, JavaScript and Python tools that support doctor routes.\n\nThe scripts validate route manifests, inspect MCP installations, audit parent skill hubs, report graph freshness, check a skill's distance from the Skilled format and convert one, read the root instruction file against the shipped template and embed or restore it, then plan and apply framework release updates. Most are read-only. The release updater's apply and rollback paths write local state, and so does the skill converter's `--apply`. Every script has a test under `tests/`, except `skill-graph-freshness.cjs`, whose freshness panel test lives in the skill advisor runtime. One runner executes them all.\n\n---\n\n## 2. DIRECTORY TREE\n\n```text\n.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/\n+-- agent-roster-mirror-check.cjs\n+-- agents-md-check.cjs\n+-- agents-md-embed.cjs\n+-- agents-md-manifest.cjs\n+-- audit_descriptions.py\n+-- command-catalog-mirror-check.cjs\n+-- check-mcp-mutation-class.sh\n+-- ci-report.cjs\n+-- ci-scaffold.cjs\n+-- ci-toggle.cjs\n+-- doctor-helper-match.cjs\n+-- doctor-phrase-reach.cjs\n+-- git-hook-gates.cjs\n+-- git-setup-check.cjs\n+-- git-standards.cjs\n+-- mcp-doctor-lib.sh\n+-- mcp-doctor.sh\n+-- opencode-version-battery.cjs\n+-- orphan-process-check.cjs\n+-- parent-skill-check.cjs\n+-- route-validate.py\n+-- route-validate.sh\n+-- release-update.cjs\n+-- sk-code-quality-config.cjs\n+-- sk-code-setup-check.cjs\n+-- sk-skill-check.cjs\n+-- sk-skill-convert.cjs\n+-- skill-graph-freshness.cjs\n+-- lib/\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"sed -n '1,60p' .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/README.markdown\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527379370,"end":1794527379866}}}}
